1
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:12,060
In a cinema, row 1 has 18 seats, row 2 has 22, row 3 has 26, and so on.

2
00:00:12,110 --> 00:00:15,820
Listing 40 rows would take a long time.

3
00:00:15,870 --> 00:00:17,970
How many seats are in row 40?

4
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:21,730
Pause the video and work it out.

5
00:00:21,780 --> 00:00:26,010
From row 1 to row 40 there are 39 steps of 4.

6
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:31,150
18 plus 39 times 4 is 18 plus 156, which is 174 seats.

7
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:34,450
A formula makes this quicker.

8
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:38,620
Remember: the Nth term gives any term from its position.

9
00:00:38,670 --> 00:00:41,930
3 N minus 1 gives 2, 5, 8, and so on.

10
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:44,840
The difference tells you the multiple of N.

11
00:00:44,890 --> 00:00:48,290
Then compare with the sequence, and adjust.

12
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:50,220
For example: 7, 10, 13.

13
00:00:50,270 --> 00:00:54,240
The difference is 3, so start with 3 N: 3, 6, 9.

14
00:00:54,290 --> 00:00:55,910
Each term is 4 more.

15
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:58,970
So the Nth term is 3 N plus 4.

16
00:00:59,020 --> 00:00:59,810
Your turn.

17
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:02,490
Find the Nth term of 18, 22, 26.

18
00:01:02,540 --> 00:01:06,140
Pause the video and try it.

19
00:01:06,190 --> 00:01:09,910
The difference is 4, so 4 N: 4, 8, 12.

20
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:11,990
Each term is 14 more.

21
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:14,770
The Nth term is 4 N plus 14.

22
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:18,000
Row 40: 160 plus 14 is 174.

23
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:23,030
Some sequences are based on N squared: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25.

24
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:27,610
For example, 2 N squared gives 2, 8, 18, 32.

25
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:28,940
Here is the method.

26
00:01:28,990 --> 00:01:31,100
Step 1: find the difference, D.

27
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:32,690
Start with D N.

28
00:01:32,740 --> 00:01:37,460
Step 2: compare D N with the sequence, and add or subtract.

29
00:01:37,510 --> 00:01:41,730
Step 3: if it looks quadratic, compare with N squared.

30
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:44,410
Step 4: check with a term.

31
00:01:44,460 --> 00:01:47,620
Find the Nth term of 40, 37, 34, 31.

32
00:01:47,670 --> 00:01:53,600
The difference is minus 3, so minus 3 N: minus 3, minus 6, minus 9.

33
00:01:53,650 --> 00:01:55,480
Each term is 43 more.

34
00:01:55,530 --> 00:01:57,460
So 43 minus 3 N.

35
00:01:57,510 --> 00:01:58,160
Your turn.

36
00:01:58,210 --> 00:02:01,480
Write the first four terms of N squared plus 3.

37
00:02:01,530 --> 00:02:04,440
Pause and work it out.

38
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:10,910
1 plus 3, 4 plus 3, 9 plus 3, 16 plus 3: 4, 7, 12, 19.

39
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:14,110
Is 100 in the sequence 4 N plus 14?

40
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:18,030
4 N plus 14 equals 100 gives N equals 21.5.

41
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:21,690
That is not a whole number, so no.

42
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:25,050
Finn finds the Nth term of 5, 8, 11, 14.

43
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:27,280
Which line is wrong?

44
00:02:27,330 --> 00:02:28,430
Line 2 is wrong.

45
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:32,930
The difference is the multiple of N, so start with 3 N.

46
00:02:32,980 --> 00:02:34,520
3 N gives 3, 6, 9.

47
00:02:34,570 --> 00:02:35,050
Add 2.

48
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:37,980
The Nth term is 3 N plus 2.

49
00:02:38,030 --> 00:02:44,780
If you have 50 dollars and save 12 dollars a week, after N weeks you have 12 N plus 50.

50
00:02:44,830 --> 00:02:48,920
Planners use Nth terms like this all the time.

51
00:02:48,970 --> 00:02:50,340
One last question.

52
00:02:50,390 --> 00:02:52,780
Which term of 2 N plus 1 is 41?

53
00:02:52,830 --> 00:02:56,300
Pause the video and answer.

54
00:02:56,350 --> 00:02:58,360
2 N plus 1 equals 41.

55
00:02:58,410 --> 00:03:00,510
2 N is 40, so N is 20.

56
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:03,020
It is the 20th term.

57
00:03:03,070 --> 00:03:06,700
To remember: linear Nth term: D N, then adjust.

58
00:03:06,750 --> 00:03:09,260
Check with the first term.

59
00:03:09,310 --> 00:03:12,220
Quadratic: compare with N squared.

60
00:03:12,270 --> 00:03:16,500
Solve an equation to test if a number is in the sequence.

61
00:03:16,550 --> 00:03:21,710
Great work! Now practise lesson 9.2 on Camday.
